1. Answer the following questions.
a. What is duck typing, as explained in the video?

Duck typing means an object can be used based on the methods it has, even if it does not belong to the same class.

b. What did the Car class need to have so it could work inside the same loop as Dog and Cat, even if it does not inherit from the Animal class?

It needed to have the same move() method as Dog and Cat.

2. Python Code

class Car:
    def move(self):
        print("The car drives on the road.")


class Boat:
    def move(self):
        print("The boat sails on water.")


class Drone:
    def move(self):
        print("The drone flies in the sky.")


def start_trip(vehicle):
    vehicle.move()


car = Car()
boat = Boat()
drone = Drone()

start_trip(car)
start_trip(boat)
start_trip(drone)

3. How does the code in 2.2 demonstrate polymorphism?
It demonstrates polymorphism because the same start_trip() function works with different objects. Each object has its own version of the move() method.
